n entities (e.g., organizations and individuals) within a network.\n\n# Goal\nWrite a comprehensive report of a community, given a list of entities that belong to the community as well as their relationships and optional associated claims. The report will be used to inform decision-makers about information associated with the community and their potential impact. The content of this report includes an overview of the community's key entities, their legal compliance, technical capabilities, reputation, and noteworthy claims.\n\n# Report Structure\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n89,INTERACTION EFFECT,\"**Interaction Effect**\n\n**Definition and Purpose**: An interaction effect occurs when the effect of one independent variable (factor) on the response variable is influenced by the level of another independent variable. This concept is crucial in the analysis of experimental designs, as it helps to understand how different factors work together to affect the outcome, rather than in isolation.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation**: In a typical two-factor analysis, the interaction effect can be represented in a linear model as follows:\n\n\\[ Y_{ijk} = \\mu + \\alpha_i + \\beta_j + (\\alpha\\beta)_{ij} + \\epsilon_{ijk} \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( Y_{ijk} \\) is the response variable,\n- \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean,\n- \\( \\alpha_i \\) is the effect of the i-th level of factor A,\n- \\( \\beta_j \\) is the effect of the j-th level of factor B,\n- \\( (\\alpha\\beta)_{ij} \\) is the interaction effect between factors A and B,\n- \\( \\epsilon_{ijk} \\) is the random error term.\n\n**Design Layout**: Interaction effects are typically assessed in factorial designs, where multiple factors are varied simultaneously. A full factorial design allows for the estimation of all main effects and interaction effects, while a fractional factorial design may be used when resources are limited.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**: The analysis of interaction effects assumes:\n1. Independence of observations.\n2. Normality of the residuals.\n3. Homogeneity of variances (homoscedasticity).\n4. Linearity of the relationship between factors and the response variable.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**: Interaction effects should be considered when:\n- There is a theoretical basis to expect that the effect of one factor may depend on another.\n- The experimental design includes multiple factors that are likely to interact.\n- The goal is to optimize a response variable based on multiple influencing factors.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**: To analyze interaction effects, one typically conducts an Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) that includes interaction terms. The significance of interaction effects can be tested using F-tests, and if significant, further investigation into the nature of the interaction (e.g., simple effects analysis) may be warranted.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**: \n- Understanding interaction effects provides deeper insights into the relationships between factors.\n- It can lead to more accurate models and predictions by capturing the complexity of real-world phenomena.\n- Identifying significant interactions can inform better decision-making in experimental settings.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**: \n- Interaction effects can complicate the interpretation of results, especially in higher-order interactions.\n- They require larger sample sizes to estimate accurately, particularly in factorial designs with many factors.\n- Overfitting can occur if too many interaction terms are included in the model without sufficient data.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**: Interaction effects are commonly analyzed in factorial designs, but can also be explored in response surface methodology (RSM) and mixed models. Alternatives include using regression analysis to model interactions in observational data.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**: When designing experiments to assess interaction effects, researchers should ensure that the levels of each factor are adequately represented and that the design is balanced to avoid confounding. It is also important to consider the potential for higher-order interactions, which may require more complex designs.\n\n**Software Implementation**: Many statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, and SPSS, provide built-in functions for conducting ANOVA and estimating interaction effects. Users can specify interaction terms in their models to facilitate analysis.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**: Interaction effects are frequently studied in various fields, including agriculture (e.g., the combined impact of temperature and pressure on crop yield), manufacturing (e.g., the interaction between machine settings and material types), and social sciences (e.g., the interaction between demographic factors and behavioral outcomes). Understanding these interactions can lead to improved processes and outcomes in these domains.\",4\r\n510,MAIN EFFECT,\"**Entity: MAIN EFFECT**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**  \nA main effect is defined as the direct effect of an independent variable (or factor) on a dependent variable (or response) in an experimental design. It represents the average change in the response variable associated with a one-unit change in the factor, while ignoring the effects of other factors in the model. Main effects are crucial for understanding how individual factors influence the outcome of an experiment.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**  \nIn a factorial design, the main effect of a factor \\( A \\) can be mathematically represented as:  \n\\[\n\\text{Main Effect of } A = \\frac{1}{n_A} \\sum_{i=1}^{n_A} (Y_{Ai} - \\bar{Y})\n\\]  \nwhere \\( Y_{Ai} \\) is the response for the levels of factor \\( A \\), \\( \\bar{Y} \\) is the overall mean response, and \\( n_A \\) is the number of observations at each level of factor \\( A \\). This formulation highlights the average effect of factor \\( A \\) on the response variable.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**  \nMain effects are typically assessed within the context of factorial designs, where multiple factors are varied simultaneously. In a full factorial design, all possible combinations of factor levels are tested, allowing for the estimation of main effects as well as interaction effects.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**  \nThe validity of main effect analysis relies on several assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations: The responses must be independent of one another.\n2. Normality: The residuals (differences between observed and predicted values) should be normally distributed.\n3. Homogeneity of variance: The variance among the groups should be approximately equal.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**  \nMain effects are applicable in experiments where the researcher is interested in understanding the individual contributions of factors to the response variable. They are particularly useful in factorial designs, where multiple factors are tested simultaneously. Selection criteria include the need for simplicity in interpretation and the desire to isolate the effects of individual factors.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**  \nTo analyze main effects, researchers typically use Analysis of Variance (ANOVA). The procedure involves:\n1. Formulating the null hypothesis that there is no main effect for the factor.\n2. Calculating the F-statistic to compare the variance between group means to the variance within groups.\n3. Interpreting the p-value to determine statistical significance.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**  \n- Main effects provide clear insights into the influence of individual factors on the response variable.\n- They simplify the interpretation of complex experimental results by isolating the effects of each factor.\n- Main effects can be easily visualized using plots, such as main effect plots.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**  \n- Main effects do not account for interaction effects between factors, which can lead to misleading conclusions if interactions are present.\n- They may oversimplify the relationship between factors and the response variable, especially in complex systems.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**  \nMain effects are often analyzed alongside interaction effects in factorial designs. Other related methods include regression analysis, where main effects can be assessed in the context of a broader model that includes interactions and other covariates.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**  \nWhen implementing main effect analysis, researchers should ensure that the experimental design is adequately powered to detect the effects of interest. It is also important to consider the number of levels for each factor, as this can influence the ability to detect main effects.\n\n**Software Implementation:**  \nMany statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, and SPSS, provide built-in functions for conducting ANOVA and estimating main effects. These tools facilitate the analysis and visualization of main effects in experimental data.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**  \nMain effects are commonly analyzed in various fields, including agriculture (e.g., studying the effect of fertilizer type on crop yield), manufacturing (e.g., assessing the impact of temperature on product quality), and psychology (e.g., evaluating the influence of different teaching methods on student performance).\",7\r\n511,DIVISOR,Divisor is a term used in the context of factorial experiments to describe the number used to calculate the mean effects and interactions.,2\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n3459,INTERACTION EFFECT,YIELD,\"The interaction effect is related to yield, as it measures the combined influence of temperature and pressure on the yield outcome\",34\r\n559,MAIN EFFECT,INTERACTION EFFECT,\"Main effects can influence interaction effects in a factorial experiment, showing how f